A modular machine-learning pipeline for identifying unusual financial transactions using statistical feature engineering and unsupervised anomaly detection.
Overview
Financial transaction systems can generate large volumes of data, making it difficult to manually identify unusual patterns.
This project demonstrates an end-to-end anomaly detection workflow that:
Generates reproducible synthetic transaction data.
Validates and cleans transaction records.
Creates statistical and behavioral features.
Uses Isolation Forest for unsupervised anomaly detection.
Calculates anomaly scores.
Summarizes detected anomalies.
Produces visualizations.
Provides automated tests for core components.
The project is designed as a portfolio-quality demonstration of data analytics, statistics, machine learning, and software engineering practices.
Problem Statement
Traditional rule-based approaches can be difficult to maintain when transaction behavior changes.
The objective of this project is to develop a reproducible system that identifies transactions that differ substantially from the general transaction population.
The system does not determine whether a transaction is definitively fraudulent. Instead, it identifies transactions that may require further investigation.
Methodology
The pipeline follows this process:
Synthetic Transaction Data
          ↓
Data Validation
          ↓
Data Cleaning
          ↓
Feature Engineering
          ↓
Isolation Forest
          ↓
Anomaly Scores
          ↓
Anomaly Classification
          ↓
Evaluation & Visualization
Features
The model uses several transaction characteristics:
Transaction amount
Transaction frequency
Transaction hour
Location change indicator
Device change indicator
Night-time transaction indicator
Combined behavioral risk signal
